WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.368 --> 00:00:02.602 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So you are doing your show
in Manhattan.

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:03.870 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes.
-But I heard word

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.572 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you are operating
out of a hotel.

00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:08.074 align:start size:94% position:6%
-We are, yes.
The Pennsylvania Hotel.

00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:10.310
-The Pennsylvania Hotel.
The historic Pennsylvania Hotel.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:12.012
-Oh, yes. Right by Penn Station.
-Yeah.

00:00:12.012 --> 00:00:14.280 align:start size:94% position:6%
-The sexiest part of
New York City.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.782 align:start size:94% position:6%
If you know anything about
New York City,

00:00:15.782 --> 00:00:17.817
you know you go to Penn Station,
you get a slice of pizza,

00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:19.452 align:start size:94% position:6%
you see some TV,
and you sleep in the bedbox.

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:21.621 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-It´s, uh, we...

00:00:21.621 --> 00:00:23.590 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Most people walk out
of Penn Station, they say,

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:25.191
"I want a hotel right here!"

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:26.526 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:26.526 --> 00:00:27.861 size:88% position:44%
-"I never want to leave!

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:30.597 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is there a place for
political satire around here?"

00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:33.666 size:75% position:38%
Yeah, we actually --
We toured the hotel.

00:00:33.666 --> 00:00:38.538 align:start size:94% position:6%
They reconfigured the ballroom
to make it a studio.

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:40.540 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we walked around,
and it had this funny smell.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:41.975
It´s like, "What is that smell?"
They´re like,

00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:43.443 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Oh, there was a food show
in here before here."

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:45.779 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was like,
"Yeah, what food show?"

00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:48.448 align:start size:94% position:6%
"The Worst Cooks"
was in here beforehand.

00:00:48.448 --> 00:00:50.417 align:start size:94% position:6%
So you -- you could imagine
the smell.

00:00:50.417 --> 00:00:52.652 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, a food show beforehand
is going to smell.

00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:55.422 align:start size:94% position:6%
"The Worst Cooks" still lives
in our studio.

00:00:55.422 --> 00:00:57.424 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah. So you have to
overcome that.

00:00:57.424 --> 00:00:59.259 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes.
-But you have a great team.

00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:00.760 align:start size:94% position:6%
You have some
"Daily Show" experience.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:02.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-On this show,

00:01:02.362 --> 00:01:04.898 align:start size:94% position:6%
you are playing a character
who is alt-right,

00:01:04.898 --> 00:01:08.401
sort of inspired by Alex Jones,
a bit of a conspiracy theorist.

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:09.702 align:start size:94% position:6%
Tell us a little bit more
about the character.

00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:12.405
-Yeah. We´re kind of diving into
this alt media landscape,

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:15.642 align:start size:94% position:6%
the world of "InfoWars"
and "Breitbart," "The Blaze."

00:01:15.642 --> 00:01:18.578
Like, we found, like, this world
of, like, paranoid conspiracy

00:01:18.578 --> 00:01:21.147 align:start size:94% position:6%
that suddenly filters its way
down into the mainstream

00:01:21.147 --> 00:01:22.849
and into the Oval Office.

00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:24.617 align:start size:94% position:6%
That was kind of the world
we wanted to live in.

00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:25.952 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, it was a good time
for this.

00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:27.887
-Yeah.
-You -- I want to talk about it,

00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:29.989 align:start size:94% position:6%
because it´s sort of inspired
by Alex Jones.

00:01:29.989 --> 00:01:32.092 align:start size:94% position:6%
And first I want to show,
here´s a promo for your show,

00:01:32.092 --> 00:01:33.660 align:start size:94% position:6%
"The Opposition."

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.395
-Who are they? The enemy.

00:01:35.395 --> 00:01:37.130
Who´s the enemy? They are.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:38.865 align:start size:94% position:6%
I already said that.
Pay attention.

00:01:38.865 --> 00:01:41.334 align:start size:94% position:6%
It´s time for us
to fight back against them,

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:44.471 align:start size:94% position:6%
because them are fighting
against we.

00:01:44.471 --> 00:01:46.406 align:start size:94% position:6%
-All right, very good.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:48.341 align:start size:94% position:6%
Made it super clear.
-Very clear.

00:01:48.341 --> 00:01:50.610 align:start size:94% position:6%
-But then -- this seems to me
like a gift.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:51.911 size:78% position:39%
Tell me if I´m wrong.

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:56.483
Alex Jones saw that promo
and did a long piece on it

00:01:56.483 --> 00:01:58.685
on his show, on his radio show.

00:01:58.685 --> 00:02:01.087 align:start size:94% position:6%
-He has a lot of content
to fill up on the radio show.

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:03.623 align:start size:94% position:6%
When you -- when you do
alt media radio,

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:05.225
you´re on air about 20 hours.

00:02:05.225 --> 00:02:07.260 align:start size:94% position:6%
So if there´s something to
talk about, you talk about it.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:10.296
-And here´s what he had to say
about that promo you just saw.

00:02:10.296 --> 00:02:12.732 align:start size:94% position:6%
-He´s up there saying,
"You gotta go through me."

00:02:12.732 --> 00:02:15.602
And then he´ll explain how
he politically operates.

00:02:15.602 --> 00:02:17.770 align:start size:94% position:6%
He´s really telling them
how to operate,

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.405 size:84% position:42%
not the way we operate.

00:02:19.405 --> 00:02:22.375 size:75% position:38%
Then he also says it
very disingenuously.

00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:23.843 align:start size:94% position:6%
So they´re there
getting their programming

00:02:23.843 --> 00:02:26.813 size:75% position:38%
on how to attack us,

00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:28.715 align:start size:94% position:6%
while they´re learning
to hate us,

00:02:28.715 --> 00:02:31.484 align:start size:94% position:6%
but really they´re discovering
their identity.

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:33.486 size:78% position:39%
This is mind control.

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:35.321 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah. Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:36.990 size:84% position:42%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.826 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So here you are,

00:02:39.826 --> 00:02:42.095 align:start size:94% position:6%
not even an episode
into your show,

00:02:42.095 --> 00:02:45.098 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you have full engagement
with your inspiration.

00:02:45.098 --> 00:02:46.766 align:start size:94% position:6%
It´s a great start.
-Locked and loaded.

00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:48.902 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he <i>is </i>right.
It is mind control.

00:02:48.902 --> 00:02:50.737 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, okay, good.
-Oh, yeah.

00:02:50.737 --> 00:02:53.072
Viacom told me, "We need to get
inside the minds

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:56.309 align:start size:94% position:6%
of 18 to 23-year-olds,
get them engaged in the show

00:02:56.309 --> 00:02:58.444
so we can sell them fizzy drinks
during the advertisement."

00:02:58.444 --> 00:03:00.313 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he knows it. He sees it.
-Fantastic.

00:03:00.313 --> 00:03:01.714 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes, yes, boom.

00:03:01.714 --> 00:03:03.816 align:start size:94% position:6%
Huh, huh, huh!

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:05.451 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yeah, he -- he knows what
he´s doing.

00:03:05.451 --> 00:03:06.886
He´s signaling me, he knows.

00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:08.421
He wants me to bring it on,
and you know what? I´m happy.

00:03:08.421 --> 00:03:09.756 align:start size:94% position:6%
-There you go.
-Yeah, uh-huh.

00:03:09.756 --> 00:03:11.257 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And now you´ve --
But genuinely,

00:03:11.257 --> 00:03:13.726
you have been engaging a lot,
or I should say not engaging,

00:03:13.726 --> 00:03:15.361 align:start size:94% position:6%
but doing a lot of research
about this world

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:17.263
that you want to talk about.

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:19.699
And what is it -- You must have
uncovered some things,

00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:22.969
some actual, genuine ways that
people interact or are drawn to

00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:24.237 align:start size:94% position:6%
the kind of media
that you´re talking about.

00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:26.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, I do think
when you get on the fringe

00:03:26.172 --> 00:03:27.874 align:start size:94% position:6%
with a lot of these
media sources,

00:03:27.874 --> 00:03:29.642
or just news in general,
I think what we´re starting

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:32.178 align:start size:94% position:6%
to respond to is how it´s not
necessarily the facts

00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:33.680 align:start size:94% position:6%
that these people are
pulling from.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.815 align:start size:94% position:6%
The veracity and validity
that they get

00:03:35.815 --> 00:03:37.750 align:start size:94% position:6%
is how they experience
the facts.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:40.186
So, like, even Alex Jones
does this on his show.

00:03:40.186 --> 00:03:41.354 size:78% position:39%
Other people as well.

00:03:41.354 --> 00:03:42.755 size:75% position:38%
It´s not about like,
"I heard this fact."

00:03:42.755 --> 00:03:44.891
It´s like, "I experienced this.
I got this e-mail.

00:03:44.891 --> 00:03:46.226 align:start size:94% position:6%
I saw this thing."

00:03:46.226 --> 00:03:48.628 align:start size:94% position:6%
So an anecdote
suddenly becomes a fact

00:03:48.628 --> 00:03:50.430 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it becomes a conspiracy
and it becomes,

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:51.965 align:start size:94% position:6%
through a little bit
of paranoia,

00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:54.067
then becomes something that
other people begin to follow.

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:57.203 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so you just start to see
this path towards chaos.

00:03:57.203 --> 00:03:59.138 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then you make a show
about it.

00:03:59.138 --> 00:04:02.475
-Yeah. In making the show
about it, does it become --

00:04:02.475 --> 00:04:04.677
Is it any less chaotic to you?

00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:07.380
Do you sort of feel like you´re
getting your head around it?

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.749 size:84% position:42%
Or in your research,
do you have a sense of,

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:11.351 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Oh, this is worse than
I thought?"

00:04:11.351 --> 00:04:12.852 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I think you start to wrap
your head around it.

00:04:12.852 --> 00:04:16.356
I mean, I would guess -- Like,
it´s cathartic to come to work

00:04:16.356 --> 00:04:17.690 align:start size:94% position:6%
and have a bunch of writers
and be like,

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.525 size:88% position:44%
"Did you see this thing?
Are you experiencing --"

00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:21.261 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, like, to be able
to hash that out

00:04:21.261 --> 00:04:23.830
and try to make sense of it
through a show later in the day,

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:26.099
I think is something that
we all gain something from.

00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:28.134 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes, you will learn,
once you start doing the show,

00:04:28.134 --> 00:04:29.669 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the off weeks are
the hard weeks

00:04:29.669 --> 00:04:30.870 align:start size:94% position:6%
´cause that´s when you will
go crazy.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:32.071 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes, yeah.
-Exactly.

00:04:32.071 --> 00:04:33.373
´Cause then the news happens,

00:04:33.373 --> 00:04:35.108 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you have nowhere to talk
about it, so you just

00:04:35.108 --> 00:04:37.010
sort of wander around Manhattan,
talking out loud.

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:38.344
-And just start screaming.

00:04:38.344 --> 00:04:39.846 align:start size:94% position:6%
That´s when I celebrate
my anniversary with my wife.

00:04:39.846 --> 00:04:41.381 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, there you go.
-Yes. Yes.

00:04:41.381 --> 00:04:42.582 size:84% position:42%
-You came from Chicago.

00:04:42.582 --> 00:04:44.150 align:start size:94% position:6%
Much like me,
you were an improviser.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:45.718 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes. Mm-hmm.
-Do you have any memories of

00:04:45.718 --> 00:04:48.087
your favorite times in Chicago,
your favorite shows?

00:04:48.087 --> 00:04:49.255 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Favorite shows?

00:04:49.255 --> 00:04:50.890
Probably my favorite improv show
in Chicago,

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:52.191 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was in a group called
"American Dream",

00:04:52.191 --> 00:04:54.227 align:start size:94% position:6%
which was a lovely group
of people.

00:04:54.227 --> 00:04:56.596 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we did a bunch
of weird, crazy shows.

00:04:56.596 --> 00:04:58.298
And the one that stands out is,

00:04:58.298 --> 00:04:59.732 align:start size:94% position:6%
one of our members
showed up late --

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:02.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
´cause every improviser
works two different jobs --

00:05:02.402 --> 00:05:04.003 align:start size:94% position:6%
was exhausted
and needed to sleep.

00:05:04.003 --> 00:05:06.039 align:start size:94% position:6%
So we did an entire show
at I.O.,

00:05:06.039 --> 00:05:08.508
where he went to sleep on stage.

00:05:08.508 --> 00:05:10.543 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we decided just to whisper
the entire show.

00:05:10.543 --> 00:05:12.045 align:start size:94% position:6%
We asked the audience
not to laugh,

00:05:12.045 --> 00:05:14.213 align:start size:94% position:6%
but to raise their hands.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:14.213 --> 00:05:15.515 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they did.

00:05:15.515 --> 00:05:17.250 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so we did this entire show
where people were,

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.018 align:start size:94% position:6%
instead of laughing,
were raising their hands.

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:20.787
And you started to see --
There was an improviser

00:05:20.787 --> 00:05:22.388
that came in halfway through,

00:05:22.388 --> 00:05:24.524 align:start size:94% position:6%
and just saw a bunch of people
raising their hands

00:05:24.524 --> 00:05:25.692 align:start size:94% position:6%
and us whispering.

00:05:25.692 --> 00:05:27.260 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was like,
"This feels like a cult."

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.295 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was an improv show,
so he was right.

00:05:29.295 --> 00:05:31.164 size:75% position:38%
-Yeah, he <i>was </i>right.

00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:32.965 align:start size:94% position:6%
Well, you´ve come a long way
from that.

00:05:32.965 --> 00:05:35.501
And congratulations.
I really can´t wait for Monday.

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:36.903 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thanks so much
for being here, Jordan.

00:05:36.903 --> 00:05:38.805
-Thank you sir. I appreciate it.
-Jordan Klepper, everybody.

